Subject: Admit lazy function bodies to the untraced set

What this does
  Widens the acyclic predicate with one term, so a function whose body has been
  deferred by the bytecode reader also qualifies.

Why we need it
  A deferred body reserves its constant pool inline from the header and skips the
  rest, so the pool count is non-zero for a pool that has never been written and
  the pool-count term excludes the objects the deferred reader creates.

What it adds
  The reserved slots are all the integer zero, because the reader's allocation
  zeroes them and nothing writes the pool before the body is materialized, so
  marking one does nothing; that is the same fact the free path already depends on.
  A body whose materialization failed is excluded, because it holds references by
  then. No new promotion site is needed: the only way out of the deferred state
  runs through the materializer, which promotes before it fills the pool. No opcode
  is added or retired and BC_VERSION is unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -6884,8 +6884,14 @@
    retain memory. */
 static int fnbc_is_acyclic(JSFunctionBytecode *b)
 {
-    return b->cpool_count == 0 && b->objlit_tab == NULL &&
-           b->ic_table == NULL;
+    /* A lazily read body reserves its constant pool inline in the same zeroed
+       allocation and never writes it until the body is materialized, so the
+       reserved slots are all JS_TAG_INT 0 and marking them does nothing.  A
+       body whose materialization FAILED is excluded: JS_ReadFunctionBody fills
+       the pool before it installs byte_code_buf, so such a body is flagged lazy
+       with real references in its pool. */
+    return (b->cpool_count == 0 || (b->is_lazy && !b->lazy_failed)) &&
+           b->objlit_tab == NULL && b->ic_table == NULL;
 }
 
